## 3. Exact Contraction Theorem

Assume `L>0` and (2). Define

\[
 r_-={1-\sqrt{1-2h}\over \Gamma L},
 \qquad q=\Gamma Lr_-=1-\sqrt{1-2h}<1.                   \tag{6}
\]

Require

\[
 r_-\le R,
 \qquad r_-<r_{\rm pos},                                 \tag{7}
\]

where `r_pos` is a certified radius on which the Hermitian form, bundle
metrics and all open algebraic constraints remain admissible.

Define the fixed-derivative Newton map

\[
 T(s)=s-A^{-1}\Phi_{\rm phys}(s).                         \tag{8}
\]

For `v=s-s0`, Taylor's formula and (5) give

\[
 \left\|A^{-1}
  \bigl(\Phi(s)-\Phi(s_0)-Av\bigr)\right\|
 \le {\Gamma L\over2}\|v\|^2.                          \tag{9}
\]

Consequently

\[
 \|T(s)-s_0\|
 \le \Gamma\eta+{\Gamma L\over2}r_-^2=r_-,             \tag{10}
\]

where the equality is the quadratic equation encoded by (6). Also

\[
 \|DT(s)\|=\|I-A^{-1}D\Phi(s)\|
 \le \Gamma Lr_-=q<1.                                    \tag{11}
\]

Thus `T` is a contraction of the closed ball into itself. Banach's fixed-point
theorem gives a unique `s*` in that ball, and invertibility of `A` turns
`T(s*)=s*` into

\[
 \Phi_{\rm phys}(s_*)=0.                                 \tag{12}
